        "text": "*[Bug 18015] tools:add_datalist silently ignores Rdata files, only accepts RData files. Proposal to allow both, or warn, or at least document*\n<https:\/\/bugs.r-project.org\/show_bug.cgi?id=18015>\n\nSebastian Meyer (<mailto:seb.meyer@fau.de|seb.meyer@fau.de>) changed:\n\n           What    |Removed                     |Added\n----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n           Severity|normal                      |enhancement\n\n--- Comment #5 from Sebastian Meyer (<mailto:seb.meyer@fau.de|seb.meyer@fau.de>) ---\nThanks for taking a look. I still think the documentation of the\n`tools::add_datalist()` build helper should *not* duplicate the list of\nsupported data formats; `?data` is the right place for this information -- with\nall the relevant details.\n\nThat said, the `\\seealso` section is now (r90199) more explicit about the\nreference:\n\n    -  \\code{\\link{data}}.\n    +  \\code{\\link{data}}, for a description of the supported data formats.\n\nMore importantly, the documentation needed clarification that 'datalist' files\nare irrelevant for packages using 'LazyData'. (@CRAN: &gt;200 packages using\n'LazyData' do supply a 'datalist' file, but that is unused and sometimes\noutdated; R CMD check should probably suggest removal.)\n\n(In reply to Simon Dedman from comment #3)\n&gt; the way it operates invisibly could be done more helpfully\n\nYes, the current return value (`NULL`) is indeed unhelpful and also not\ndocumented. I think that `add_datalist()` should at least report if it actually\ndid write `data\/datalist`.  It could be made a logical value and then also be\nused by R CMD build to report the result of:\n\n    * looking to see if a 'data\/datalist' file should be added",
