        "subtype": "bot_message",
        "text": "*[Bug 19091] as.numeric on macOS aarch64 rounds \"0.9999999999999999\" to 1.0 instead of the nearest IEEE 754 double*\n<https:\/\/bugs.r-project.org\/show_bug.cgi?id=19091>\n\n--- Comment #6 from Suharto Anggono (<mailto:suharto_anggono@yahoo.com|suharto_anggono@yahoo.com>) ---\n(In reply to Akitada Kasahara from comment #0)\nI think `R_strtod5()` is the culprit. It uses `LDOUBLE`. I guess this happens\nwhen `long double` is the same as `double`.\n\n    for ( ; *p &gt;= '0' &amp;&amp; *p &lt;= '9'; p++, ndigits++) ans = 10*ans + (*p - '0');\n    if (*p == dec)\n        for (p++; *p &gt;= '0' &amp;&amp; *p &lt;= '9'; p++, ndigits++, expn--)\n            ans = 10*ans + (*p - '0');\n\nAfter the above, `expn` is `-16` and `ans` is supposed to hold\n`9999999999999999`. When `long double` is the same as `double`,\n`9999999999999999` cannot be represented exactly and gets rounded instead to\n`10000000000000000` (`10^16`), exactly.\n\n    LDOUBLE p10 = 10., fac = 1.0;\n\nBecause `expn &lt; 0`,\n\n        for (n = -expn, fac = 1.0; n; n &gt;&gt;= 1, p10 *= p10)\n            if (n &amp; 1) fac *= p10;\n        ans \/= fac;\n\nis done. `fac` is `10^16`, exactly.",
